Desktop TUN on Linux routes through policy rules and a table of its own,
the way sing-tun's auto_route does, instead of routes in the main table.

autoSystemRoutingTable put its routes in main. Those routes also answer the
kernel's reverse path check, so with net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=1 (a common
hardening setting) every reply from the proxy server arriving on the physical
interface had its source routed back into the TUN and was dropped: the core
started, and nothing went through.

Now the routes go to table 2026 and rules 9100..9110 lead to it: the layout of
sing-tun's rules() without uid or interface filters, as mihomo and sing-box
use it, plus one rule for every uid but root. A lookup without a source yet
(any unbound socket), any socket of a user process, forwarded traffic, and DNS
to any address reach the table. The reverse path check looks up as root with
iif lo and the packet's local address as source, so it stays on main and
passes. A socket bound to the physical interface finds no route in the table
for that interface and falls through to main, as before.

The uid rule is what keeps a browser's WebRTC inside: it binds a socket to the
address of every network, and without the rule that STUN request carried a
source past every source rule and showed the real address. The price is the
one the main table routes had: replies of a server run by a user to a client
from outside go into the TUN; root's servers (sshd) answer the way the request
came. Telling those apart takes conntrack marks, which xray does not set.

The tun gets rp_filter=2 for itself, as sing-tun does. Rules in the range are
removed before they are added, so a crashed run leaves nothing that the next
one keeps. Linux desktop only: Android gets the TUN descriptor and never adds
routes, and Windows keeps its own code.
